{
  "id": "proj-parallettes-pair",
  "slug": "parallettes-pair",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Parallettes Pair",
  "summary": "Low push-up and L-sit bars from PVC. Cheap, light, and surprisingly solid.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 2,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Cut the two 5 ft pipes with the ratcheting cutter: two 20 in handles, four 6 in uprights and eight 5 1/2 in feet. The cutter leaves no kerf, so the cut list fits in 10 ft with a few inches to spare.",
    "Sand a light chamfer on the outside of every cut end and clear the burrs from inside so the pipe seats fully in the fittings.",
    "Dry-fit one bar: handle into two elbows, an upright down from each elbow into the middle of a tee, and a foot into each side of the tee, capped at the ends. Set it on a flat floor and check that it does not rock. Mark alignment lines across every joint.",
    "Working one joint at a time in a ventilated space, apply primer then cement to both the pipe and the socket. Push the pipe home with a quarter turn to your alignment mark and hold it for 30 seconds. Keep both tees parallel so the bar stands flat.",
    "Build the second bar the same way and compare it with the first one standing next to it.",
    "Let the cement cure for the time on the can, normally 24 hours, before you put weight on the bars.",
    "Wrap the handles in cloth hockey tape for grip. Load the bars gradually, starting with push-ups before L-sits. Static holds are the job here; do not jump onto them."
  ],
